#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087623 :question/id #uuid "770866d5-545f-5ab7-a48e-df9ca76f248e" :question/math-academy-id "e-7312" :question/problem "Suppose that the value $V(t)$ of a particular car can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} = -0.1V,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the car was first purchased. If the car was purchased for $\\$10\\,000,$ what will it be worth after $3$ years?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the right-hand side of the equation is *negative*, which means that the car's value is *decreasing.*\n\nThis is an exponential decay differential equation with $r = 0.1.$ Therefore, the general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nV(t) = V_0 e^{-0.1 t}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the car was first purchased for $\\$10\\,000.$ Therefore, $V_0 = 10\\,000,$ and we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nV(t) = 10\\,000 e^{-0.1 t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the car's value after $3$ years, we substitute $t=3$ into the particular solution above. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(3) & =10\\,000{e}^{-0.1\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =10\\,000{e}^{-0.3} \\\\  & ≈7\\,408.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the car will be worth approximately $\\$7\\,408$ after three years."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088008 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4514e843-dc0c-5be5-9e96-42ad1bfc870e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{v+1}{{v}^{2}+1}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272978} {:answer/id #uuid "680783c7-a79b-52f6-8c63-e5a4705ea03c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{v+1}{2{v}^{2}-v}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273285} {:answer/id #uuid "7a0d77cc-d328-5023-810b-e3a1f565a8d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{2{v}^{2}-v}{v+1}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273437} {:answer/id #uuid "b9914a60-1aeb-5bf6-9bfb-f20416011265" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{v+1}{2{v}^{3}-{v}^{2}}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273999} {:answer/id #uuid "f794ae15-8082-5a6e-b5d7-76a88fb45ece" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{{v}^{2}+v}{3{v}^{2}-v}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274558}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186273285} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7c0796e-4620-5dae-9cfd-a686aee98f07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "695b49d1-9327-503a-b244-1f7f385f59ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-14315" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3{y}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}+y{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIf the transformation $y=v(x)x$ is applied to the differential equation, what is the resulting equation?" :question/worked-solution "Using the transformation $y=vx$ (where $v=v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given differential equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3{y}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}+y{x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{3(vx)^{3}}{x(vx)^{2}+(vx){x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{3{v}^{3}{x}^{3}}{v{x}^{3}(v+1)} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{3{v}^{3}\\cancel{{x}^{3}}}{v\\cancel{{x}^{3}}(v+1)} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3{v}^{2}}{v+1}-v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3{v}^{2}}{v+1}-\\frac{v(v+1)}{v+1} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3{v}^{2}-{v}^{2}-v}{v+1} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2{v}^{2}-v}{v+1} \\\\ \\left(\\frac{v+1}{2{v}^{2}-v}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088016 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "105f8391-3fbf-5ab7-8db9-35a36af25c39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "206" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272533} {:answer/id #uuid "6e03f6f7-3628-5b3e-9581-17f220b4c60f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273344} {:answer/id #uuid "a532aeb0-2eb0-5f16-8159-14fac09b2e90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273814} {:answer/id #uuid "d731d7c9-c734-503d-8e73-75cd7ecfbf19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274267} {:answer/id #uuid "f34da9b9-f946-5d5a-b05c-8f534792f44f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "403" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274536}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186273814} :answer-field/id #uuid "d29cb716-a820-5cd3-bdc1-78be482514eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275007}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "884109e1-5e14-5c1c-933b-9800a24392f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-15208" :question/problem "The number of particles $N(t)$ in a radioactive substance after $t$ years can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=-3N.\n$$\n\nIf there were $8\\,500$ particles initially, calculate the number of particles remaining after $2$ years. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We model the number of particles in the substance using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=-3N.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nN(t)={N}_{0}\\,{e}^{-3t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${N}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that there were $8\\,500$ particles initially, so we have ${N}_{0}=8\\,500.$\n\nTherefore, the number of particles $N$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nN(t)=8\\,500\\,{e}^{-3t}\\,.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the number of particles when $t=2,$ we substitute $t=2$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(2) & =8\\,500\\,{e}^{-3\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =8\\,500\\,{e}^{-6} \\\\  & ≈21.069\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding our answer to the nearest integer, we conclude that $21$ particles remain."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088017 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f433f265-720b-5ad5-a34a-5f846dd719d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36 \\mathrm{\\% }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084837} {:answer/id #uuid "1518bb64-4c55-5b14-9cb4-3ebe25445cce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "48 \\mathrm{\\% }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084838} {:answer/id #uuid "2bd6c57d-f3d9-5265-b7cc-db4cfb4b5acd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "43 \\mathrm{\\% }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084839} {:answer/id #uuid "443937d6-85f3-57de-9ca5-4a1ff51f71b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "33 \\mathrm{\\% }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084840} {:answer/id #uuid "47cafe7f-580b-5617-8e98-efe9980a0748" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "46 \\mathrm{\\% }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084841}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7b23276d-3d1e-5d30-b106-5875efa12113" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084836}] :question/id #uuid "ae0a168d-3a0a-5ef4-9fe5-c80d5bbfdc08" :question/math-academy-id "q-15219" :question/problem "The number of particles $N = N(t)$ in a radioactive substance after $t$ years can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$\\frac{dN}{dt} =-kN$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is the decay rate. If there were $230000$ particles initially, and $N(5) = 23000$, calculate the value of the decay rate $k$ to the nearest percent.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088122 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97b2bb68-89cb-59c8-9356-f76f21ec7260" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293529}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293529} :answer-field/id #uuid "0415426c-2704-586e-93d9-ec13672b3656" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293943}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d11d0095-5740-5d82-8a0d-f23a175699b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-279153" :question/problem "![](images/b1/b12eb6672d263655f9f77eaafbf486a7be5bf9b7673c73fd0d75382d192a658b.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=\\frac{3}{2}$ and the vertical radius has length $b=\\frac{9}{2}.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{9}{4}\\right)}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{81}{4}\\right)} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{81} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088321 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b14229b-7244-5a13-b9f7-377caf1b3763" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$6$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342721} {:answer/id #uuid "5f7b224c-d2f2-57f2-923d-636856ca9541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$8$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343046} {:answer/id #uuid "909cdc5c-8c31-567d-a8db-9603fcfc60fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$6$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343502} {:answer/id #uuid "abc3321f-0ab1-56d9-b892-997b85188d6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$4$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343749} {:answer/id #uuid "cdb1fa09-1293-5ba1-af7a-c2963a194695" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$8$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344052}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343046} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3d2e838-6d36-58ab-9502-f89e82764f33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344893}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b586f348-117c-513c-b7f9-86528f54bc6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-7598" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=4$ and ${b}^{2}=16.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=4,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{4}=2.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=16,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{16}=4.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=4,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=2.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 4=8,$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 2=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088322 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e887380e-c6a2-55c5-a953-644c684c42f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$6$ and $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086587} {:answer/id #uuid "022e8e85-f2f9-5f22-89c3-32299f61eba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{10}$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086588} {:answer/id #uuid "25b8aed4-093a-5a57-ad29-a6f742a77db5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$6$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086589} {:answer/id #uuid "37b46b49-d523-50f1-9995-b5a05ce4f820" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{10}$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086590} {:answer/id #uuid "60da5fd4-8377-57c4-87c2-582cca3b9c69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$5$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086591}] :answer-field/id #uuid "dc165d99-699e-586e-b731-222ff1843dbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086586}] :question/id #uuid "256fdb5e-1d5d-5f2a-8bad-6f30d3b32832" :question/math-academy-id "q-7600" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation\n$\\frac{x^{2}}{10} + \\frac{y^{2}}{9} = 1$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088323 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f245cc5-cf66-5e23-941f-5ed3c61f8519" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{7}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342368} {:answer/id #uuid "2aaf0f54-99ea-5f51-b032-c17b629e54df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342595} {:answer/id #uuid "b0519dd2-e0e8-5e54-915c-ce7ec5a247b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{24}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343788} {:answer/id #uuid "f625dd86-0749-5723-adf5-e39041ad4f8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344405} {:answer/id #uuid "fb39f685-9e2f-50ea-ae5a-250fc73349a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344448}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344405} :answer-field/id #uuid "65e8416d-99db-5904-9535-5182a7fbb499" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344665}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0f8eff0d-f84c-5907-a2c9-a5a7eb91d57f" :question/math-academy-id "q-7603" :question/problem "![](images/8a/8ac809353e7834d328d645fafb5c02e17386ba087e778a841865ef6313893e79.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=\\frac{7}{2}$ and the vertical radius has length $b=2.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3.5}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{{7}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}\\right)}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{49}{4}\\right)}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088324 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17dbb695-a8da-50c8-bbc0-8305a0e101a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342448} {:answer/id #uuid "2fb68ca0-59ba-5dad-94a0-0b73ef26ecfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342636} {:answer/id #uuid "99b15d26-b6be-5ef3-a9cf-fd12150a374e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343589} {:answer/id #uuid "a9d29486-cbd3-56cc-baa6-eb5af1fe093e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343737} {:answer/id #uuid "bb629a7c-214b-5b78-9be1-51f1b708557d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343886}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342636} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cdedc79-da92-5992-bc36-02e000bd1509" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344689}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "808bb938-3f2e-5c94-82a4-205181f5d6ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-7604" :question/problem "![](images/d2/d2c35e72acad77ae747c6a78c9794e9b076c047891d4b12d5c9b3176bdc6dde5.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=4$ and the vertical radius has length $b=2.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088325 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92d9a932-277e-5479-a0ef-d4e0d662da4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x^{2}}{9} + \\frac{y^{2}}{4} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086605} {:answer/id #uuid "9e1b8dd5-2fdf-5ae1-b381-7df76cf18c0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x^{2}}{16} + \\frac{y^{2}}{9} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086606} {:answer/id #uuid "18331ea6-dd35-58c4-a87b-66e41b8a0158" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x^{2}}{3} + \\frac{y^{2}}{4} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086607} {:answer/id #uuid "70187839-c8fc-50ea-8e78-c929f95a7f65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x^{2}}{9} + \\frac{y^{2}}{16} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086608} {:answer/id #uuid "7f852fe4-272e-5d76-b674-33cd9f6c8153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x^{2}}{4} + \\frac{y^{2}}{3} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086609}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f771dc03-583e-530f-908d-e69dc267d68e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086604}] :question/id #uuid "f0ef5818-9fef-50e7-a456-afae14c51c79" :question/math-academy-id "q-7605" :question/problem "![](images/df/dfda198c093371945608318353c997435f2d388ca62f94cc3d27f49cfaf14cb8.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088414 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3495ebe4-0ff8-587e-946b-39cf32f7c349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\$355$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348920} {:answer/id #uuid "585d5ff7-c990-5b8e-9c9c-dbc4951b081f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\$586$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349258} {:answer/id #uuid "647c1398-390e-5363-ba09-9403a7d4d31e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\$125$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349368} {:answer/id #uuid "b77d1897-d9d4-571e-809b-923026590486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\$216$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350070} {:answer/id #uuid "bb175228-e457-5110-b2df-5ff95ab0825b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\$966$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350110}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349258} :answer-field/id #uuid "2564adfc-5914-509e-b0ca-e7985013c023" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6894d124-282d-5217-8531-e4a1976ca68b" :question/math-academy-id "q-83140" :question/problem "The outstanding amount $L(t)$ that Daphne needs to repay the bank $t$ years after taking out a loan can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}=-0.5L.\n$$\n\nIf she borrowed $\\$32\\,000,$ calculate the debt remaining after $8$ years. Round your answer to the nearest dollar." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We model the amount of debt remaining using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}=-0.5L.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nL(t)={L}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.5t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${L}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that $\\$32\\,000$ were borrowed, so we have ${L}_{0}=32\\,000.$\n\nTherefore, the amount of debt $L$ remaining at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nL(t)=32\\,000\\,{e}^{-0.5t}\\,.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the amount of debt remaining when $t=8,$ we substitute $t=8$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(8) & =32\\,000\\,{e}^{-0.5\\cdot 8} \\\\  & =32\\,000\\,{e}^{-4} \\\\  & ≈586.100\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding our answer to the nearest integer, we conclude that $586$ dollars of debt remain."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088415 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "859719b6-a3bc-50c8-b7e7-6f37c499f024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$308$ feet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087144} {:answer/id #uuid "ade18a31-835b-5c80-8fae-2648aa6d4458" :answer/type 