00843c-69fa-53d1-90b1-8fb33e4a7492" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18 \\mathrm{\\% }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087154}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7d0980f1-97e0-5d72-83c0-38efd6dcbcef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087149}] :question/id #uuid "abd6cc5e-f20b-59da-8471-8a236b147a77" :question/math-academy-id "q-83151" :question/problem "The value of a particular investment $V = V(t)$ after $t$ years can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$\\frac{dV}{dt} = kV$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is the growth rate. If the investment was worth $\\$4800$ dollars initially, and $V(3) = \\$7200$, calculate the growth rate $k$ to the nearest percent.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088421 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bed0619c-b398-5e69-bc05-ed10de39c428" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\$313327$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087180} {:answer/id #uuid "e4ed0554-5e15-5993-97e5-f96a72145355" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\$306194$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087181} {:answer/id #uuid "12189597-9767-5a87-9e82-729b70e54aae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\$453125$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087182} {:answer/id #uuid "3f494756-1ffe-535a-8718-45f4609d2b56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\$526149$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087183} {:answer/id #uuid "52a922fd-78ad-51e2-8611-1e7f29e6cbc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\$563384$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087184}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4a53a407-9d7d-5dce-b894-7698f291feae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087179}] :question/id #uuid "73ec1591-5dcd-58e6-bc37-ede70a24c3c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-8363" :question/problem "Due to inflation, the price of a particular property $P(t)$ after $t$ years can be modeled by the differential equation\n$\\frac{dP}{dt} = 0.0325P$.\n\nIf this property's price is currently $\\$250000$, calculate its price after $25$ years. Round your answer to the nearest dollar.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088437 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b387a19d-7cf7-5c7c-b4a0-3c531f2dc207" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{x^{2}y}{x^{2} + y^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087276} {:answer/id #uuid "c5fc4204-9ff1-5434-b8c8-b243a9b5a4ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{2xy}{x^{2} + y^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087277} {:answer/id #uuid "e8891a45-78f7-5a8b-a02d-8cc9d4f8af08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{x^{2}y^{2}}{x^{2} + y^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087278} {:answer/id #uuid "37572047-280a-508a-ac11-6a5fe1712a8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{xy^{2}}{x^{2} + y^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087279} {:answer/id #uuid "7253b068-0bec-5847-aaa9-da6e7449baf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{xy}{x + y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087280}] :answer-field/id #uuid "fc06f993-8bb8-5bba-9060-505b57e636e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087275} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6836dad2-0a5e-566e-82e6-ae2f1e00252c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395952} {:answer/id #uuid "02b3bcfe-b179-54e6-9f39-7cf2b8a2012c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{xy}{x+y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395953} {:answer/id #uuid "501f1379-0915-537f-a01b-ac50f840b151" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395954} {:answer/id #uuid "f65a218e-09b9-5f93-833d-5222a9ab1b89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395955} {:answer/id #uuid "9438294d-1900-5c07-88ff-915745bfe633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395956}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395955} :answer-field/id #uuid "f23bfdfe-607b-5b9b-8499-14f49fb7f966" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395951}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "32964a8b-4376-5c16-9f30-00e530a869bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-8739" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order homogeneous ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if it satisfies the following property:\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)=f(x,y)\\,\n$$\n\nThe only option in which this is the case is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe can check this using one of two methods.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nLet $f(x,y)=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$\n\nWe see that both the numerator and denominator are homogeneous polynomials of degree $2.$ Therefore, $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree $2-2=0.$\n\nHence, the original differential equation is homogeneous.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo check this in detail using the definition, we first write:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nWe can check that this is homogeneous showing that $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{2(tx)(ty)}{(tx)^{2}+(ty)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}\\cdot 2xy}{{t}^{2}{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{{t}^{2}\\cdot 2xy}{{t}^{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y),$ the original differential equation is homogeneous."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088438 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36ee7f59-7955-5e6d-97a3-31d520538549" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352078} {:answer/id #uuid "a4501cde-34bb-5948-b58a-a742171c4d99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352970} {:answer/id #uuid "bd5c2162-7781-5446-ba96-31792294d28e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353181} {:answer/id #uuid "d6a895b9-f9ad-59f2-9ce7-b2f5eae6b151" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353426} {:answer/id #uuid "f282b685-5577-5d72-ac2d-ce3ef3882ac2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353683}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353181} :answer-field/id #uuid "f08fe0a1-984f-586e-afda-76db1f02247e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354234}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8c38fa75-d73d-55f8-bbc1-1dea4b79194b" :question/math-academy-id "q-8741" :question/problem "Which of the following is NOT a first-order homogeneous ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if it satisfies the following property:\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)=f(x,y)\\,\n$$\n\nThe only option in which this is NOT the case is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x.\n$$\n\nWe can check this using one of two methods.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nLet $f(x,y)=x.$\n\nWe see that it is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree $1.$\n\nHence, the original differential equation is  NOT homogeneous.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo check this in detail using the definition, we first write:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\,\n$$\n\nWe can check that this is not homogeneous showing that $f(tx,ty)\\ne f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =tx=tf(x,y)\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(tx,ty)\\ne f(x,y),$ the original differential equation is NOT homogeneous."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088439 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fb6dd3b-cac4-56a1-b66d-59181029c178" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{v}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351700} {:answer/id #uuid "6c07fbd9-126c-50e4-9dbf-421b28f03956" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2v+\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{v}\\right)}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352523} {:answer/id #uuid "b595eb22-625b-5bb1-8e42-847d5c60fc39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{v}\\right)}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353123} {:answer/id #uuid "d4a7e3b8-ab6a-52d1-a9b2-9dae8f6322f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{v}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353410} {:answer/id #uuid "d6351ed0-f215-5d36-b70b-cf1350e332e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{v+\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{v}\\right)}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353422}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351700} :answer-field/id #uuid "d40102e8-bbfb-507e-87af-b7854b0ffc32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354175}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6aa0e045-65b8-5bd8-8a66-10e8e133beb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-8745" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right)+\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nIf the transformation $y=v(x)x$ is applied to the differential equation, what is the resulting equation?" :question/worked-solution "Using the transformation $y=vx$ (where $v=v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given differential equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right)+\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{vx}\\right)+\\frac{vx}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{v}\\right)+v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{v}\\right) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{v}\\right)} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{v}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088444 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f095d9d-73ca-5510-90d9-13b6f42d391c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y^{2} = Kx^{4} - 3x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087318} {:answer/id #uuid "14ce0339-1417-5b9c-b34f-15d48bba12ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y^{2} = Kx^{3} - x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087319} {:answer/id #uuid "23493c53-f790-52b7-8d4b-a2a247c6a8df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y^{2} = Kx^{3} + 2x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087320} {:answer/id #uuid "3c5f3ce3-6b89-5309-ab95-65da4990d433" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y^{3} = Kx^{3} - x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087321} {:answer/id #uuid "5e069116-da75-542a-b369-bd8e185c2346" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y^{2} = Kx^{4} + x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087322}] :answer-field/id #uuid "6f5a5051-ab3a-58d2-839d-37c5299136cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087317}] :question/id #uuid "ecc5b0a4-8e5a-5917-a8c3-d4ab3b2b520d" :question/math-academy-id "q-8843" :question/problem "Use the transformation $y = v(x)x$ to find the general solution of the following homogeneous differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{x^{2} + 3y^{2}}{2xy}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088445 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "033b5955-1071-5d95-b2f7-c2bd33d3768d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(C{x}^{3}+x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351594} {:answer/id #uuid "16da157c-25e8-539b-972d-839e07401032" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-{x}^{3}+C{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351768} {:answer/id #uuid "44d00c81-de3d-54d8-a887-f6cce43dbe9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+C{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352200} {:answer/id #uuid "5ec6f001-1f40-5e1d-8eaf-f6d17c1f89cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{3}={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+Cx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352407} {:answer/id #uuid "d8863625-e75e-5388-909d-087df892fe66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}+C{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353447}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353447} :answer-field/id #uuid "3026f036-9a48-51a7-b01d-a4f3f08da8e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353897}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "19530196-afb6-5c10-98a9-988afc75a28c" :question/math-academy-id "q-8844" :question/problem "Find the general solution of the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{xy}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This is a homogeneous equation because it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y),\n$$\n\nand we notice that all terms in the numerator and denominator of $f(x,y)$ are of order $2$ (i.e., they are quadratic).\n\nUsing the transformation $y=v(x)x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{xy} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{(vx)^{2}-{x}^{2}}{x(vx)} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{x}^{2}({v}^{2}-1)}{v{x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{v}^{2}-1}{v}-v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{v}^{2}-1-{v}^{2}}{v} \\\\ v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int v\\,\\text{d}v & =-\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}}{2} & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+2{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we introduced $C=2{C}_{1}.$\n\nFrom the transformation $y=vx$, we get\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above into our solution, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)}^{2} & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}+C \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}+C{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092116 :question/id #uuid "c986ab8f-a8a6-5721-bc8d-ba7ec19b95ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-727" :question/problem "Calculate $f'(x)$ if $f(x) = e^{5x +4}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf = e^u, \\qquad u=5x+4.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = e^u,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =5.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =5{e}^{u} \\\\  & =5{e}^{5x+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092118 :question/id #uuid "775dfc95-df31-5198-aeb2-4603b8386957" :question/math-academy-id "e-728" :question/problem "Given that $y= 3\\cdot 2^{1 - x},$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = 3\\cdot 2^u, \\qquad u=1-x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = 3\\cdot 2^u\\ln(2),\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =-1.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {2}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {2}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092119 :question/id #uuid "b15a88b5-e2c4-51c1-90d5-031c2a8c23d3" :question/math-academy-id "e-729" :question/problem "Given that $y=e^{-x^2},$ calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = e^u, \\qquad u=-x^2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = e^u,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =-2x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (-2x) \\\\  & =-2x{e}^{u} \\\\  & =-2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092512 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6fe2c4c-f454-5aca-a824-b3a7d3b7906a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{3x}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089244} {:answer/id #uuid "fd2c856f-0818-5870-a81c-c09468b81451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "xe^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089245} {:answer/id #uuid "33420c54-a5e9-5e50-8d8a-2b748db05ce4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3e^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089246} {:answer/id #uuid "43e58a07-4775-5ba0-87bf-4177453776ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3xe^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089247} {:answer/id #uuid "62505802-48e7-5721-a94d-ba4a709ec693" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089248}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c07e8ff0-97b2-55ed-aa61-6034627f1aa5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089243}] :question/id #uuid "2b5accbf-e263-55a8-aa47-3d5a01076c05" :question/math-academy-id "q-11816" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = e^{3x}$, find $\\frac{df}{dx}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092513 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4726f486-296b-54df-b7de-616f90a2a430" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2x+5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524505} {:answer/id #uuid "8de0ee3e-9434-591e-863f-19dc03481eb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x{e}^{2x+5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524606} {:answer/id #uuid "ac9861ac-ab1f-5a28-b691-91302c31566a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{2x+5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524659} {:answer/id #uuid "cafdcf0f-5e0a-5f8f-978e-3e3f2f8dc403" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x+5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524715} {:answer/id #uuid "fc809e37-d2bf-588c-ad7b-ff6395a75916" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2x+5){e}^{2x+5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524836}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524659} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa7337e6-4d8a-5b6d-9fd4-37daba6415d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b27b93f8-5f19-566f-95e3-f0a0b21ec684" :question/math-academy-id "q-11817" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={e}^{2x+5},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={e}^{u},\\,u=2x+5.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2{e}^{u} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x+5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092618 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "265b541b-62a2-540d-bd79-5ef733742b3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x{e}^{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285123} {:answer/id #uuid "4a81ce5d-011d-51ee-bf9c-6333bf4dc66f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285478} {:answer/id #uuid "4b97230b-e5a8-5328-9497-45595399fb66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285492} {:answer/id #uuid "812af0ad-7d34-5c50-a85a-78aa2431dcbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x{e}^{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285975} {:answer/id #uuid "dedd5724-68fc-54e4-9b62-8ebbbd324624" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286818}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285975} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cfdfe24-c411-57d2-aa42-0fbbe96ad9c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0e52fa16-cf3e-5339-95e2-786fe7ef7e5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-1953" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)={e}^{{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={e}^{u},\\,u={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (2x) \\\\  & =2x{e}^{u} \\\\  & =2x{e}^{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092733 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d9ee625-e773-554a-b6fd-1b00c950d2f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2 \\cdot 3^{2x}\\ln (3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090599} {:answer/id #uuid "a2e4f469-6799-5ce4-b565-a59f324b5ec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4x3^{2x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090600} {:answer/id #uuid "bdacec05-5c20-55b6-8105-e82bd5158002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x3^{2x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090601} {:answer/id #uuid "f7139b42-c4a4-5c78-ab9a-4c8bcf3ecb8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4x3^{2x^{2}}\\ln (3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090602} {:answer/id #uuid "15755bfa-20fa-599b-b783-c67a495db7ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x3^{2x}\\ln (3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090603}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4acd11bc-cb2e-5a8e-a9ee-06ec6470c2d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090598} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c382a73-b479-5088-ba73-8174216969b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4x{3}^{2{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402979} {:answer/id #uuid "7bd5037d-c800-5b92-94d5-bf8378100f05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x{3}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402980} {:answer/id #uuid "bc15acd8-2e05-54df-824b-49b27e8976d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4x{3}^{2{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402981} {:answer/id #uuid "c86614c3-2ff7-5322-a6c3-9ce930da4214" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x{3}^{2{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402982} {:answer/id #uuid "098f7a16-97a6-5c7b-bd17-6d5da322792f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\cdot {3}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402983}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402979} :answer-field/id #uuid "08bb9372-bdf9-5f57-a4fb-f3aaaf1824d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8711f34a-9cc5-5a70-9af6-219e0592f067" :question/math-academy-id "q-48565" :question/problem "If $f(x)={3}^{2{x}^{2}},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={3}^{u},\\,u=2{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}={3}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={3}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)\\cdot (4x) \\\\  & =4x\\cdot {3}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\  & =4x\\cdot {3}^{2{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092734 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f53b1fe-17ee-5800-9a3f-82327590a8b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4x^{5}e^{x^{4} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090605} {:answer/id #uuid "b344b273-973c-5cd4-9b78-f9f4b6c14df2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4x^{3} + e^{x^{4} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090606} {:answer/id #uuid "16fb416e-e5bf-57d4-a129-dd88c945f90c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4x^{3}e^{x^{4} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090607} {:answer/id #uuid "2ec445b1-9e9b-5639-9cba-48edb379db01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4x^{4}e^{x^{4} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090608} {:answer/id #uuid "63133f5d-41f8-5703-8b59-5583d2d92a0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4x^{5} + e^{x^{4} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090609}] :answer-field/id #uuid "566e0008-fcd5-5a48-a5f1-0d9f404686db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090604}] :question/id #uuid "842ba2f6-4002-59f4-86c1-997627e51415" :question/math-academy-id "q-48628" :question/problem "Given that $y = e^{x^{4} + 1}$, find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092735 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa029f20-9e8b-5be2-9aaf-e25b51c039d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(12x)3^{6x^{2} - 4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090611} {:answer/id #uuid "adc2037a-76a7-570a-a5b0-aad7e20b5c7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(12x)3^{6x^{2} - 4}\\ln (3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090612} {:answer/id #uuid "cbe2a81f-2507-5d07-bb73-29c3696d08a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(12x - 4)3^{6x^{2} - 4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090613} {:answer/id #uuid "d255dee3-7ee2-5cfc-af90-221a41a1961e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(12x - 4)3^{6x^{2} - 4}\\ln (3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090614} {:answer/id #uuid "582b9da7-3b84-58f5-a115-eedbe6fbcb2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(12x^{3})3^{12x}\\ln (3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090615}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d7d3b7cd-8fef-5ba2-8969-6b86fbad9f15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090610}] :question/id #uuid "475f4a17-648a-5df1-a5af-24c4bc3218f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-48631" :question/problem "Given that $y = 3^{6x^{2} - 4}$, find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096400 :question/id #uuid "19bf8d06-cbcc-547b-b882-ac89cd39fd1a" :question/math-academy-id "e-181" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = (3x^4+x^3)^5,$ calculate $\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf = g^5, \\qquad g = 3x^4+x^3\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g}  = 5g^4,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x} = 12x^3 + 3x^2.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =5{g}^{4}\\cdot (12{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =5(3{x}^{4}+{x}^{3})^{4}(12{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096401 :question/id #uuid "8fcdb2c8-71ca-5bfa-b7fe-49f8f2c05bbb" :question/math-academy-id "e-182" :question/problem "Given that $y = \\sqrt{1-x^2},$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}.\n$$\n\nHere, we've used $u$ instead of $g$ for the intermediate variable. \n\nLet $u=1-x^2.$ Then $y = \\sqrt u = u^{1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = \\dfrac{1}{2}u^{-1/2} = \\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt u}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}  = -2x.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (-2x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{2\\sqrt{u}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
